From: Nikita Popov <nikita.ppv@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Jun 2020 22:08:54 +0200
Subject: Use sha256 as digest algorithm in certificate generator

This makes the generated certificates compatible with security
level 2, which is apparently the default on Ubuntu 20.04.

Unfortunately this does not fix all tests, because some are using
pre-generated certificates.

(cherry picked from commit 52c2f1126d762385118284cf35b99e02b7ce8afe)
(cherry picked from commit 81de8314dd4fd4daa7a04409e286d32e0168826b)
(cherry picked from commit 68d3fca41bf0d9f0e4bfa8191c6d343ba7c91892)
(cherry picked from commit 26c5fab4cc618fd27ce2a25c7de51afe634275f8)
(cherry picked from commit 1b4747aaf59aef9f5a4e1a9eab9b23daa9360aa4)
---
 ext/openssl/tests/CertificateGenerator.inc | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/ext/openssl/tests/CertificateGenerator.inc b/ext/openssl/tests/CertificateGenerator.inc
index 325f975..e915e81 100644
--- a/ext/openssl/tests/CertificateGenerator.inc
+++ b/ext/openssl/tests/CertificateGenerator.inc
@@ -97,7 +97,8 @@ class CertificateGenerator
             openssl_csr_new($dn, $this->lastKey, ['req_extensions' => 'v3_req']),
             $this->ca,
             $this->caKey,
-            2
+            /* days */ 2,
+            ['digest_alg' => 'sha256'],
         );
 
         $certText = '';
